    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter August 24 with a clear focal point in **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (82.13), who headlines our lineup against Merrill Kelly. His elite sprint speed profile combined with a favorable 5.27 xERA matchup makes him our strongest start, bolstered significantly by a platoon advantage and a leadoff spot that maximizes his potential to manufacture runs and steal bases. **Junior Caminero** (76.13) also projects as a high-upside play; despite a difficult assignment against Framber Valdez, the wind-aided park factors and his presence in the #3 hole provide sufficient offensive lift to keep him as an essential anchor in our 5x5 build. Meanwhile, **Lars Nootbaar** (60.62) provides a sneaky source of value against Kevin Gausman, as his dynamic platoon splits effectively offset the challenging SP matchup, keeping him well within the \"Strong\" start threshold.\n\nThe most difficult decision on today\u2019s slate involved the benching of **Jacob Gonzalez** (42.41). While his matchup against Robbie Ray presented a respectable SP skill advantage, his projected #8 spot in the lineup and a challenging L/L platoon disadvantage suppressed his efficiency score just enough to keep him behind our middle infielders. **Trea Turner** (51.35) and **Kyle Schwarber** (50.87) sit in the \"Marginal\" range today, as they face the high-level arm of Logan Gilbert; we are keeping them active primarily to maintain volume, but they remain at risk of underperforming against a pitcher with a 3.46 xERA. Managers should closely monitor the official lineups for **Colt Keith** and **Caleb Durbin**, as their scores are currently based on projected order; any drop in their batting positions would necessitate a re-evaluation of their utility against Rasmussen and Alcantara. With **Yordan Alvarez** and **Kazuma Okamoto** both sidelined by schedule gaps, the burden is on **Pete Crow-Armstrong** and **Junior Caminero** to carry our stolen base and RBI production today.",
